<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ma Huan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1380–c. 1460</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ma Huan was a Chinese Muslim interpreter and traveller who accompanied three of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-treasure-voyages">Ming treasure voyages</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zheng-he">Zheng He</a> in the early fifteenth century. His book Yingya Shenglan, &quot;The Overall Survey of the Ocean's Shores&quot;, completed around 1451, describes the countries visited, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calicut">Calicut</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hormuz">Hormuz</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>, and is a major source for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> world of his time.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ma-huan.jpg" alt="Ma Huan" width="320" height="282" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ma Huan (馬歡) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yingya_Shenglan_(Jilu_Huiban),_first_page.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
